# Uyiz admin panel — environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. On Vercel these go in the
# project's Environment Variables, not in a file.

# Where the API lives. The `/api/v1` suffix is PART OF THIS VALUE: the http
# client appends bare endpoint paths like `/admin/stats` to it. Leaving the
# suffix off produces 404s on every screen with no other symptom.
#
# This variable is compiled into the browser bundle (NEXT_PUBLIC_), so it is
# public by design. Never put a server secret behind a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://maklersizkvartira-production.up.railway.app/api/v1

# Reminder, because it is the other half of making this panel work at all:
# the panel calls the API cross-origin from the browser, and the backend's
# CORS allowlist forbids "*" in production. The domain this panel is served
# from must be added to CORS_ORIGINS on the API side (Railway) or every
# request fails preflight with an opaque network error.

# ─── Admin login second factor (2FA) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS SECTION IS OPTIONAL. The panel logs in without any of
# them. That is not laziness, it is the lesson of 06.09.2026: these three used
# to have hardcoded fallbacks — a live bot token, the real operations group and
# a signing key spelled out in the source — and when the fallbacks were removed
# (rightly: a key in a public repository lets anyone forge the challenge
# cookie) the routes began answering 503 {"error":"2fa_not_configured"}.
# Nothing had ever been set in Vercel, so the login form died at step 1 and the
# only people who could have set these variables were locked out of the only
# panel from which they could be set. A second factor must never be the reason
# nobody can get in.
#
# So the routes degrade instead of refusing. What actually happens when a value
# is missing is spelled out under each one, and it is always visible: the
# function log says loudly what was skipped and why.
#
# Server-side only: no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so these never enter the browser
# bundle. Set them in the Vercel project's Environment Variables.

# STRONGLY RECOMMENDED. The key the one-time challenge is sealed with
# (AES-256-GCM, via HKDF) before it is handed to the browser as a cookie. Must
# be at least 32 characters; anything shorter is treated as absent.
#
# Generate one, do not invent one:   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Without it the routes do NOT stop. They fall down a ladder:
#   1. SECRET_KEY                            — this, the intended key
#   2. Vercel's deployment identifiers       — VERCEL_DEPLOYMENT_ID, else
#      VERCEL_URL + VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA. Vercel injects these into every
#      function of one deployment, so both routes derive the same key and the
#      panel stays reachable. WEAKER: these are not secrets. They are not in
#      this repository, which is the point, but anyone who knows the deployment
#      can reconstruct the key, and it rotates on every deploy — a challenge in
#      flight across a redeploy just asks for a new code.
#   3. a per-process random key              — development only; in production
#      the second factor is skipped instead, because two serverless instances
#      would not share it and the code could never be verified.
# Rung 2 or 3 writes an error line to the function log naming the rung and
# telling you to set SECRET_KEY. Set it and the ladder stops at rung 1.
SECRET_KEY=

# OPTIONAL. The bot that delivers the code. Create it with @BotFather and add
# it to the group below AS AN ADMINISTRATOR — a bot that is only a member
# cannot post to a supergroup.
#
# Falls back to TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN (the project's general ops bot) if that name
# is set on this deployment. Prefer a dedicated 2FA bot: it can be revoked
# without silencing the operations notifications.
#
# WHAT IS LOST WITHOUT IT: the second factor is SKIPPED, not enforced.
# /api/auth/2fa/send answers 200 {"ok":true,"twoFactorRequired":false} and the
# login page signs the operator in on the password the backend already
# verified. The panel is then protected by username and password alone. The
# same thing happens when the Telegram send itself fails — a code that cannot
# be delivered is a code nobody can type.
TELEGRAM_2FA_BOT_TOKEN=

# OPTIONAL. The group the code is posted to, WITH its leading minus (e.g.
# -1001234567890). A positive id is read by the Bot API as a user id and every
# send fails with "Bad Request: chat not found" — this project has lost a day
# to exactly that, so an id of 13+ digits beginning "100" gets its minus put
# back automatically, the same rule as normalise_chat_id in
# backend_python/app/core/config.py. An @channelusername is passed through.
#
# Falls back to TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHANNEL_ID, then TELEGRAM_GROUP_ID.
#
# WHAT IS LOST WITHOUT IT: exactly as above — the second factor is skipped and
# the panel is protected by username and password alone.
TELEGRAM_2FA_CHANNEL_ID=

# A note on what 2FA here is and is not, so the skip above is read correctly:
# these routes are defence in depth, not the gate. The backend issues the
# tokens and has no second factor of its own, so anyone holding the password
# can call the login endpoint directly and never touch them. Skipping the
# factor costs a layer; refusing cost everyone the panel.
